WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.900
Hey there! Welcome to Models &amp; Agents for Beginners Episode 113. It's July 25th, 2026.

00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:11.520
Let's break down today's coolest AI news so anyone can understand it.

00:00:11.820 --> 00:00:18.040
Let's go! A student just proved you can create an entire 100-page comic on a cheap phone using

00:00:18.040 --> 00:00:25.620
only chat GPT. The creator tested this for months with a story called Tinky and Boca, a post-apocalyptic

00:00:25.620 --> 00:00:31.800
road tale. Most people run into the same wall when they try AI for art, characters change their

00:00:31.800 --> 00:00:37.160
faces or clothes from one image to the next, and backgrounds shift like someone moved the whole

00:00:37.160 --> 00:00:43.680
scene overnight. This creator solved it with one steady rule. Every prompt asks for a complete

00:00:43.680 --> 00:00:49.600
finished page instead of a single panel, and it always repeats the exact same character and setting

00:00:49.600 --> 00:00:55.100
details. Think of it like handing an artist the same reference photo and lighting notes before

00:00:55.100 --> 00:01:00.980
each new drawing. The AI stops guessing and starts treating those details as fixed rules.

00:01:01.600 --> 00:01:06.560
That approach turns random doodles into something that feels like one connected world across

00:01:06.560 --> 00:01:12.720
dozens of pages. For anyone who loves comics or graphic novels but does not have art classes at

00:01:12.720 --> 00:01:19.180
school, this opens a new door. You can build a short story for a class project or just for fun,

00:01:19.180 --> 00:01:26.640
all on a regular phone. The creator's bigger plan is a full 100-page book made entirely on a 99-dollar

00:01:26.640 --> 00:01:33.200
Android phone, showing you do not need fancy gear to tell longer visual stories. You can test the

00:01:33.200 --> 00:01:39.900
same method yourself right now, open chat GPT on your phone, describe one character in detail the

00:01:39.900 --> 00:01:46.200
first time, then keep reminding it to use that exact description for every full page. Start with

00:01:46.200 --> 00:01:51.240
just three pages and notice how the faces and clothes stay consistent instead of drifting.

00:01:51.760 --> 00:01:57.480
Okay, now for my favorite part of the show, where we look under the hood at how this actually works.

00:01:58.220 --> 00:02:02.660
You know how when you are texting with the same group of friends every day, your phone starts

00:02:02.660 --> 00:02:08.760
remembering the way you usually talk and suggest words that sound like you. Now picture the same idea

00:02:08.760 --> 00:02:15.100
but with pictures instead of words. The AI keeps a running list of every detail you have already shown

00:02:15.100 --> 00:02:21.460
it about a character's face, clothes and the world around them. Each new page prompt includes those

00:02:21.460 --> 00:02:26.960
details again, so the model treats them like fixed rules instead of starting fresh every time.

00:02:27.700 --> 00:02:33.100
That list of details works like a shared notebook the AI checks before drawing anything new.

00:02:33.740 --> 00:02:39.040
When the notebook stays the same across prompts, the characters stop changing randomly and the story

00:02:39.040 --> 00:02:44.940
feels like one connected world. The real magic is that the AI is not remembering like a person.

00:02:45.600 --> 00:02:49.760
It is just getting the same instructions repeated so it follows the same pattern.

00:02:50.620 --> 00:02:56.220
Once you see it that way, keeping a comic consistent stops feeling like magic and starts feeling like

00:02:56.220 --> 00:03:02.660
careful note-taking. The notebook idea also explains why the full page trick works better than asking

00:03:02.660 --> 00:03:08.660
for single panels. The AI gets the whole set of rules in one go instead of piecing things

00:03:08.660 --> 00:03:13.820
together later. That simple shift in how you talk to the model is what turns it from a random

00:03:13.820 --> 00:03:20.640
doodler into a reliable storytelling partner. Meta AI just learned to check your calendar and run real

00:03:20.640 --> 00:03:26.860
tasks for you. It can now look at your schedule, plan events and do deeper research while you steer

00:03:26.860 --> 00:03:32.640
the conversation in plain language. Think of it like having a helpful assistant who actually gets things

00:03:32.640 --> 00:03:39.180
done instead of just chatting back and forth. Anyone who uses Instagram, WhatsApp or Facebook can

00:03:39.180 --> 00:03:45.040
try it today because the same Meta AI already lives inside those apps. Go to any of those apps,

00:03:45.240 --> 00:03:50.860
tap the Meta AI button and ask it something like, help me plan a study session next week using my

00:03:50.860 --> 00:03:56.000
calendar. Watch how it suggests times and even creates a simple schedule you can edit.

00:03:56.700 --> 00:04:02.060
Claude's voice mode now runs on the company's most capable models. It can read your Gmail,

00:04:02.060 --> 00:04:08.280
Google Calendar and Slack while talking with you and it is the only major voice assistant that can

00:04:08.280 --> 00:04:14.320
actually write and send an email just from your spoken instructions. Imagine talking to a friend who

00:04:14.320 --> 00:04:19.160
can pull up your notes and turn them into a finished message without you typing a single word.

00:04:19.840 --> 00:04:24.740
If you like talking instead of typing, this makes the experience feel more natural for school

00:04:24.740 --> 00:04:31.200
projects or creative work. You can try it on the Claude app on your phone. Just start a voice chat and

00:04:31.200 --> 00:04:37.220
ask it to help draft something or check your schedule out loud. Chat GPT can now search your Gmail,

00:04:37.600 --> 00:04:44.060
Slack and Drive. A new work feature lets the AI look through your email, messages, and files to

00:04:44.060 --> 00:04:49.420
answer questions like, what did my teachers say about the history project? It turns scattered

00:04:49.420 --> 00:04:55.480
information into quick answers without you digging through folders yourself. Open weights models keep

00:04:55.480 --> 00:05:00.780
getting attention from people who want to use powerful tools someone else already paid to train.

00:05:01.180 --> 00:05:06.860
It is like borrowing a really good bike instead of buying one. You get the ride without the full cost

00:05:06.860 --> 00:05:13.460
and that's a wrap. If any of today's stories made you go, huh, that's cool. Go play with it. Curiosity

